Recommended Thesis Outline: Supercritical Bound States and Vacuum Polarization in Strong-Field QED

Core Research Question:
How do finite nuclear size, nuclear charge distribution models, and non-perturbative one-loop vacuum polarization (Uehling and Wichmann–Kroll terms) shift the critical nuclear charge \(Z_{\text{cr}}\) where the \(1s_{1/2}\) electron bound state dives into the negative-energy Dirac continuum?

Phase 1: Single-Particle Radial Dirac Solver (Months 1–3)

• Physics Goal: Solve the radial Dirac equation for an electron in a central nuclear potential V(r) for both point-like (Zα < 1) and finite-size nuclei (Zα ≥ 1).
• Numerical Method: Discretize the coupled first-order radial Dirac equations using B-spline basis functions (the Dual Kinetic Balance / DKB method avoids spurious states) or a fourth-order Runge–Kutta shooting method.
• Java Implementation: Use a standard linear algebra library like EJML (Efficient Java Matrix Library) or Apache Commons Math to solve the generalized matrix eigenvalue problem Hψ = Eψ.
• Self-Verification Benchmark: Match your point-nucleus eigenvalues against the exact Sommerfeld fine-structure formula and reproduce Greiner’s classic critical charge value (\(Z_{\text{cr}} \approx 173\)) for a uniformly charged spherical nucleus.

Phase 2: Perturbative & Exact Vacuum Polarization (Months 4–7)

• Physics Goal: Compute the one-loop QED vacuum polarization potential \(V_{\text{VP}}(r)\), separating the linear Uehling term from the higher-order nonlinear Wichmann–Kroll term.
• Numerical Method: Implement numerical quadrature (Gauss–Legendre quadrature in Java) for the Uehling integral, then construct the radial Dirac Green's function from your Phase 1 B-spline basis to evaluate the exact all-orders Wichmann–Kroll charge density via partial-wave summation.
• Self-Verification Benchmark: Check that your Wichmann–Kroll results reproduce known values for heavy hydrogen-like ions (such as U⁹¹⁺ at Z=92) before pushing into the supercritical regime (Z = 170–180).

Phase 3: Original Parameter Exploration & Thesis Writing (Months 8–12)

• Original Contribution: Once your solver is verified against benchmark ions, explore an open systematic question that does not require new QFT formalism:
	• Nuclear deformation & skin thickness: Compare \(Z_{\text{cr}}\) and vacuum polarization energy shifts across realistic Fermi vs. deformed nuclear charge distributions.
	• Supercritical resonance tracking: Track how the \(1s_{1/2}\) and \(2p_{1/2}\) states enter the continuum and compute the resonance width (spontaneous positron emission rate) using complex scaling or phase-shift analysis.
• Deliverable: A clean, object-oriented Java simulation package accompanied by convergence studies (basis size, partial-wave cutoff \(\kappa _{\max }\)) and a complete master's thesis.

